**The improvement on long sequences.**
The benefits of hyperbolic space in OOD settings have been observed empirically in many works [2, 6, 10-12], but, to the best of our knowledge, a clear explanation of this effect has not been found yet. We have a hypothesis regarding this commonly observed effect which we will try to formalize using some strong assumptions here. Our goal is not to form a rigorous proof, but to provide an intuition as to why we believe this to indeed be a result of hyperbolic geometry. 

For this discussion, we will assume that:
1. We have either a Euclidean or hyperbolic model with a representation space in which instances of each class $1, \ldots, C$ are contained to a bounded ball $B_i^M = \\{x \in M : d_M(x, c_i) < r_i\\}$, where $M \in \\{ \mathbb{R}^n, \mathbb{D}^n \\}$;
2. These balls have equal radii ($\forall i, j: r_i = r_j$) and their centers have equal distance from the origin ($\forall i, j:||c_i||_M = ||c_j||_M$);
3. The balls containg each class' representations do not overlap ($\forall i, j: B_i^M \cap B_j^M = \emptyset$), so the model can properly separate classes;
4. When the model attempts to represent an instance of class $i$ from an out-of-distribution sample, such as a long sequence, the result is contained to a ball $\tilde{B}_i^M$ centered at the same $c_i$, but for some radius $\tilde{r}_i \geq r_i$, where the size of the difference between the 2 radii is determined by the specific distribution shift.

Under these assumptions, OOD samples become problematic for the model, once the $\tilde{r}\_{i}$'s become large enough that the $\tilde{B}\_{i}^M$'s start to overlap, since the model will then no longer be able to perfectly discern between instances of all classes. In hyperbolic space, these $\tilde{r}\_{i}$'s can be larger than in Euclidean space, which is somewhat easily shown through an example.

Suppose we have 4 classes, in 2-dimensional hyperbolic or Euclidean space and suppose that for each class $i$ we have evenly placed centers such that $||c\_{i}||\_{M} = 2$, with $r_i = 1$. For instance, in $\mathbb{R}^2$ we could have $c_1 = (2, 0)$, $c_2 = (0, 2)$, $c_3 = (-2, 0)$ and $c_4 = (0, -2)$ or in the Poincaré disk $\mathbb{D}\_{1}^2$ we could similarly have $c_1 = (\tanh(1), 0)$, $c_2 = (0, \tanh(1))$, $c_3 = (-\tanh(1), 0)$ and $c_4 = (0, -\tanh(1))$. Now suppose we have some distribution shift such that only $\tilde{r}\_{1}$ is larger than $r_1$. Then, you can compute how large the difference $\tilde{r}\_{1} - r\_{1}$ can become before the model can no longer distinguish perfectly between classes. For the Euclidean case, this is $d_{\mathbb{R}}(c_1, c_2) - (r_1 + r_2) \approx 0.828$, while for the hyperbolic case we get $d_{\mathbb{D}} (c_1, c_2) - (r_1 + r_2) \approx 1.34$. This shows that the hyperbolic model, with a similar organization of its representation space but a different geometry, is robust to larger distribution shifts. As the representations cover larger areas of the space, this difference becomes more pronounced due to the increased effect of the negative curvature in hyperbolic space. We hope that this artifical example can provide some intuition as to why we believe that the OOD improvements can be attributed to the hyperbolic geometry.
